Matt Weiner <m...@mattweiner.net> wrote:
>
> It seems to me that there's an argument that your shirt should be the
> first thing you enchant, if you're not playing a 'caster who'll need a Helm
> of Brilliance, or someone who'll need Gauntlets of Dexterity.* Namely,
> enchantments are just as effective no matter what armor they're on,
> and you're never going to want to change your shirt (unless you find
> a shirt that's already enchanted, and that's highly unlikely, though I
> did find a +1 shirt in one game). Also it doesn't matter if your shirt
> gets burnt (if they even burn), since it already has 0 natural AC.

A teeshirt costs 3 so it's cheap.  It weighs 5 so it's light.  It does
not hurt pricing so I will use it to replace a Hawaiian shirt.  It
fills an inventory slot that has no competition other than the
Hawaiian shirt.  It does not suffer damage because it's protected
by the body armor.

To me that's twice the list of justifications I need to purchase
an item.  If I see a teeshirt in a shop and I can tell it's not
cursed, I buy it or get my pet to steal it.  It can be enchanted
at my liesure.  I'll enchant it very early on - Fooproofing items,
brilliance and dexterity often but not always get priority.

> *Actually, more experienced hackers can answer me this:
> Do you ever really need gauntlets of dexterity? By the time
> most of my characters would be in position to drop a lot of
> enchantments on GoDex, they never miss anyway. Is
> there any other big advantage to them?

Need?  Not in combat.  But hitting every time is nice enough
that I do not mind having it redundantly.  Also early in the
game if I get gloves of dexterity I may not be hitting every
time yet.

Dexterity also effects locking/unlocking, playing some music,
and various other small benefits.  None of these seem to me
as important as the spellcasting interference by gaunlets of
power.  Lacking a disadvantage starts to matter when you
have the option of the disadvantage.

Given the choice between dexterity and leather, I'll go with
dexterity for this assortment of reasons.  If my leather is already
highly enchanted the dexterity gloves go in my bag for future
enchantment and it might be very late in the game before I switch
to them.

Given the choice between dexterity and power, I'll take dexterity
most of the time because of the spell casting difference.  Before
having strength intrinsic of 18/** I might chose power and keep
dexterity in my bag so again it might be very late in the game
before I switch to them.

Except for a wizard who already has a cornuthaum, a helm of
brilliance nearly always wins over other types of helmet.  Again
if my intrinsics already have me maxxed out in spell casting
it doesn't suck to have redundancy there.  I have played games
where I deliberately chose a helm of telepathy.  Like the choice
between power and dexterity, the choice between brilliance and
telepathy is not unambiguous.

I've played a valk with helm of brilliance, dexterity gloves, robe,
GDSM, teeshirt, boots of speed. "oR.  Works great and water
levels get frozen paths.  In the bag are armor like mummy
wrapping, telepathy, boots of jumping and levitation.  Power
doesn't even always win for combat wombat classes.


